   "flags": {},
   "order": 0,
   "mode": 0,
   "title": "An image -> terrain (read me first)",
   "properties": {
    "text": ""
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "Lifts a landscape image (a photo or an AI-painted perspective view) into a top-down height field, then feeds the terrain chain.\n\n**Pick your own landscape in Load Image first**: it opens on ComfyUI's example.png, a cartoon figure against a flat sky - lifted, the sky turns into a wall at the back and the figure into a spike. Wanted: ground running into the distance, a camera looking slightly down, a real horizon.\n\nBy default it uses Depth Anything V2 (comfyui_controlnet_aux, the first run downloads the weights automatically): it outputs **relative inverse depth**, determined only up to scale and shift; Shift and FOV X are guesses - guess wrong and the terrain gets bent, not uniformly scaled.\n\nThe more accurate route is MoGe (built into ComfyUI core): camera-space points, metric scale and a sky mask are all provided. Put the MoGe model into models/geometry_estimation/, select the two greyed-out MoGe nodes below and press Ctrl+M to unmute them - the wires are already connected: while muted, the frontend drops inputs connected to muted nodes entirely, so HeightField from Depth uses depth; after unmuting, moge_geometry takes priority.\n\nHeightField from Depth's mask layer marks which cells were actually measured; places behind the mountains that can't be seen are filled by push-pull, i.e. guessed. The world_size output is already wired downstream, so the scale matches automatically."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
